<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jingzhou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jingzhou is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Near it lie the ruins of Ji'nancheng, usually identified as Ying, the capital of the State of Chu, and the area is rich in Chu tombs. In later centuries Jingzhou was a strategic fortress of the middle Yangtze, fought over in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a>, and its Ming-era city walls survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jingzhou.jpg" alt="Jingzhou" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Popolon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muraille_Est_de_Jingzhou.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jingzhou is a prefecture-level city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> province, on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a>. It covers 14,067 square kilometres on the Jianghan Plain, in the middle reaches of the river, and is crossed by a dense network of waterways and lakes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a>, the provincial capital, lies downstream to the east, and Yichang, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-gorges">Three Gorges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> lie to the west. The central urban area grew out of Shashi City and Jingzhou Town, which was also known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a>. The city is named after the ancient province of the same name, one of the nine provinces of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which took its name from the nearby Jing Mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited for roughly 5,000 to 6,000 years, and the Daxi culture lived in present-day Jingzhou. Situated in the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, it has been a strategic military location since ancient times. The State of Chu was founded here, and Ying, an ancient city within the borders of present-day Jingzhou, became its capital in 689 BCE and remained so for over 400 years, through the Spring and Autumn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States periods</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> era the area was known as Nanjun. Guan Yu lost the city to Eastern Wu in the Three Kingdoms period, which gave rise to the modern phrase dàyì shī Jīngzhōu, carelessness lost Jingzhou. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> it was the southern capital, Nandu. Jiangling was later the capital of the Southern Qi and Liang dynasties. In July 1949 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> took the area, and in 1994 Jiangling County and Shashi City were merged into the city of Jingsha, which was renamed Jingzhou in 1996.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Numerous sites survive from the Chu period, including the ruins of five Chu cities, 73 sites with Chu culture and more than 800 ancient tombs, among them those of 18 Chu kings. Sites from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a> include the Wulin Battlefield, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-red-cliffs">Battle of Red Cliffs</a> took place, and the Huarong Path. The city walls are 9 metres high and 10 metres thick, with a perimeter of 10.17 kilometres. The Jingzhou Museum displays a well-preserved male corpse about 2,000 years old, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">lacquerware</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jingzhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son King Wen (r. 689–677 BCE) moved the capital to Ying, usually identified with the walled site of Ji'nancheng near <b>Jingzhou</b> in Hubei, although the early Ying may have lain elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
